nt to continue this practice as  a state of allowing people who serve as  lawmakers to also essentially work in  positions where they're  enforcing those laws furthering those  laws  and do we want to continue to have a  legislature that may be conflicted  because their  employers come to the legislature asking  for things  mccarty is a former eight news now  i-team reporter now the case is going to  head back to the lower court and will  decide the bigger question no matter  what happens though it is likely we'll  get appealed to the state supreme court  a federal judge says he will decide in  the next few weeks if the execution of  zane floyd is going forward floyd was  convicted of killing three people and  sentenced to death in 2004 the judge in  the case says key questions about the  execution method are still unanswered a  supply of sedatives expired at the end  of february and state detention  officials  they say they cannot get more there's  also no active death warrant on the case  right now the judge says he'll make a  decision by may 16th  dealing with the border next on politics  now the role a public health emergency  is playing in our current policy there  and the debate over whether it should  stay or go  now  you're watching politics now  relations between the u.s and russia are  at their lowest point in years and at  the heart as you can imagine is the war  in ukraine still a dramatic prisoner  swap between both the u.s and russia was  pulled off this week the u.s was given  back marine veteran trevor reed he spent  nearly a thousand days in russian  custody after being convicted for  assaulting someone while drunk an  allegation he denies in exchange the u.s  released a russian who had served 20  years for drug smuggling reid's family  says the exchange was worth it  president biden saved our sons alive  today a perfect mother's day almost as  good as the day he was born  the u.s is still working to free other  americans in custody in russia including  women's basketball player brittany  griner  the debate over immigration policy  heating up in washington d.c a federal  judge recently blocked the bite  administration from ending a president  trump era policy called title 42 rashad  hudson has that story  late monday a federal judge threw a  wrench in the government's plan to lift  title 42 through a temporary restraining  order missouri attorney general eric  smith says it was the right decision  it's a big win i think for border  security and the rule of law the trump  air policy allows migrants seeking  asylum to be expelled through a pandemic  health policy louisiana senator john  kennedy and senate republican leader  mitch mcconnell say there's no reason to  make a change one of the most foolish  ideas coming from an administration that  has become famous for foolish ideas  pulling back the title 42 authorities  will only make it  dramatically worse  it's not just republicans who are  raising concerns about changes to the  policy democrats are also warning the  administration about rolling it back  west virginia senator joe manchin also  wants it to remain in place and  basically protect our people and  citizens united states and making sure  the people coming are coming for the  right reason but ohio senator sherrod  brown says title 42 is no longer needed  because the country is at a different  place  in the pandemic we have had  pretty incredible success in fighting  this pandemic  and getting shots in people's arms the  future of the policy will likely be a  major topic when homeland security  secretary alejandro mayorkas testifies  to the house judiciary committee later  this week reporting in washington rashad  hudson  all democrats in nevada's delegation are  against lifting title 42 until more  permanent plans in place they are in  favor though of getting rid of it  eventually politics now is back after  this  [Music]  three three three three  [Music]  this week in what to watch potentially  raisi